# SVE Meta-License v5.5
### Universal Human Heritage & Reciprocity License — Consolidated Anti-Enclosure Edition

> **Maxim I.** The fruits, for all Humanity, must be good — measured continually, on different cycles; some ripen slower.
>
> **Maxim II.** Live by your skills and talents — not by your secrets.

*v5.5 **extends and envelops** v5.0 (which extends v4.0, which extends v1.3 = CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 + SVE Addendum). It repairs the §3→§4 dead-branch cascade of v5.0, narrows §1's overreach to what copyright can actually hold, and adds fifteen protections: interim-amendment legitimacy, standalone validity independent of any prior version's fate, a no-veto Contribution Rule, Tool & Synthesizer Independence (an AI provider's own logs cannot substitute for the Registry as a defense), explicit Latent Activation with channel-independence (piracy does not exempt), an AI-training/TDM closure with a discovery duty and an explicit closed-system clarification (reading is free; incorporation while closed is a full breach, with no new status-based exception to Universal Access), the Natural-Person Licensee Rule, the Wholeness Clause, the Ethical-Use Floor, absolute Universal Access with no fallback downgrade ever available for it, an End-User Fruits Duty, a Research Integrity & Truth-Bearer track, Reciprocal-Audit-Priority, an explicit Asymmetric Rigor principle (easy for the compliant, uncompromising for the rest), and a No Secondary Secrecy rule closing confidentiality/NDA workarounds. It **cannot weaken** §0 (Fruits Test) or the Core Principles of Meta-License v4.0 — it only strengthens and repairs. **Authoritative language: English.***

---

### In Plain Language *(illustrative only — §0–§14 below control if anything here reads differently)*

If you are new here: **you may use anything in the SVE Corpus — code, texts, ontologies, models, data — for any purpose, including to make money, without asking anyone first.** **Just touching, downloading, or running it already puts you inside this License — no signature required — and it makes no difference whether you got it from the official Registry or from someone who passed it along without authorization; a pirated copy binds you exactly like a licensed one, just as a pirated film doesn't exempt the viewer from copyright.** In exchange: **whatever you build with it has to stay just as open** — you cannot take a piece private, publish half and hide half, or wrap it in a closed product. **A product or service built on it must be open to every person on Earth, with zero exceptions for nationality, politics, or sanctions** — "the sun rises on the evil and the good" (Matthew 5:45), and so must you; the only lawful way to withhold it from one is to withhold it from everyone — and if you genuinely can't, there's no fallback license that lets you keep running it anyway while excluding them; you simply can't run it under this License there at all. **Only a real human being can hold this License** — a company can use it, but only through one named person who is personally, permanently on the hook, with no corporate shield. **Whoever uses the result is expected to use it for good** — bad fruit is grounds for the same audit as anything else. **If you do research, you must publish your field notes — successes and failures alike — and never fake a result**; investigators and journalists doing public-interest work get the same deal for free. **Training an AI model on this counts as building with it, too** — the model must be disclosed like any other derivative, and finding out later that it happened doesn't make it go away; it starts the clock on fixing it. **Reading it — even with a closed AI or a closed search engine — costs nothing and isn't restricted; the disclosure duty kicks in only once a system starts training on it or running on it, and from that point it applies the same way to everyone, closed or open.** **Your own private life is your own business, but the moment keeping something secret would mean hiding it from even one other person who's touched this Corpus through you — a client, an employer, a service's users — that isn't a smaller violation, it's a sign you shouldn't be using this Corpus at all.** **This License stands on its own** — even if a court somewhere throws out an earlier version, this one doesn't fall with it. **Even the Author's own earlier disclaimer of ownership doesn't undermine it: either the underlying authorship right was never waivable in the first place, or, if the disclaimer itself is invalid, everything reverts fully to the Author — both roads support this License.** **If your hands are clean, this License is almost frictionless; if they're not, every safeguard here applies at full strength and none of your past good conduct buys a discount** — that asymmetry is deliberate, and it's meant to correct, not merely punish. **Some uses are banned outright, no matter how much you pay or how transparent you are** — weapons, mass surveillance, deception at scale, harming children. And **if you ever accuse someone of breaking these rules, you go through the same check yourself, first.**

---

### §0 — Continuity & Authority
**0.1 Authority to issue.** This License is issued by the Author & Initial Custodian (Dr. Artiom Kovnatsky) under the **Declaration of Interim Custody v2.0**, pending the SVE DAO or a functioning Veche — the same authority already exercised for v5.0 and the SVE-Unicorns Patch v1.0. It is operative now, between the Custodian and any adopter, by Latent Activation, and awaits canonical ratification once a Veche exists. Removing v5.0 §3's Non-Commercial fallback (see §10) is **not** a Ratchet violation: NC was never among the immutable Core Principles, it conflicted with v4.0 §4/Appendix B's own Open Usage Right *and* with the SVE-Unicorns Patch's own requirement that Founder-Mode ventures be "a long-term, genuinely profitable business" — this version resolves that standing contradiction in favor of the earlier, foundational grants.

**0.2 Standalone validity (independent of prior versions).** §0.1 explains why sole-signature issuance is legitimate as a matter of this framework's own internal governance — but this License's validity does not ultimately depend on that story at all. It derives directly and independently from the Author's own, original, unalienated authorship and copyright in the Corpus Artifacts as their sole creator, not from the validity of v1.3, v4.0, v5.0, or the SVE-Unicorns Patch v1.0. Those instruments are cited here for continuity, historical record, and the Ratchet Principle's comparison baseline — never as this License's source of authority. If any prior version, or any provision of one, is anywhere held invalid, unenforceable, procedurally defective, or improperly issued, that holding does not affect, impair, or in any way taint this License: v5.5 stands as a fresh, self-sufficient grant and unilateral covenant in its own right, wherever necessary severable in its entire validity from the fate of any predecessor instrument.

This standalone footing is not disturbed by the Author's own disclaimer of "ownership rights" under the Declaration of Interim Custody v2.0 §§2, 5a, which states the Author "retains no ownership rights — only authorship and the duty to safeguard methodological integrity." That disclaimer renounces proprietary, exclusive, for-profit ownership — holding the Corpus as closed private property — not the underlying personal authorship itself. In many jurisdictions (for example, Germany, UrhG §§11–14; Estonia, AutÕS §12; France's *droit moral* tradition) an author's moral rights — authorship, integrity, and the right to determine the terms on which a work is first communicated to the public — are inalienable by law and cannot be waived even by the author's own declaration; a purported waiver of them is void to that extent, and the underlying rights persist and continue to support this License regardless of what the disclaimer accomplishes as to ownership. Where a jurisdiction does not recognize inalienable moral rights of this kind — this varies, see "Left for Counsel" in the companion document — the same question resolves the same way from the other direction: if the ownership disclaimer is instead held invalid or ineffective there, the disclaimed rights revert to the Author as sole original rightsholder, which independently supports this License under ordinary copyright, without reliance on the custodial framework at all. Either branch validates v5.5; there is no branch that does not.

**0.3 Contributions carry no veto.** Anyone who submits a contribution, fix, comment, pull request, or similar input toward the Corpus or this License thereby grants the Custodian, irrevocably and unconditionally, a perpetual, worldwide license — and, to the extent the contribution is itself copyrightable, an assignment — sufficient to incorporate it under SVE terms. This applies whether the contributor is a natural person or submits on behalf of an entity: accepting a contribution is not the same as that party holding a license under §2, and creates no Sponsor requirement. Submitting a contribution does not confer co-authorship of this License, a veto over its governance, or any right to demand its withdrawal, amendment, or non-distribution — governance and amendment remain governed solely by §0.1 above and, once constituted, the Veche.

**0.4 Tool & Synthesizer Independence.** Formulating, drafting, refining, or proofreading a Corpus Artifact with the assistance of an interactive software tool, computational assistant, language model, or compiler — regardless of provider — conveys no proprietary interest in that Artifact to the tool's developer or operator. Under the ordinary terms of service such providers themselves publish, rights in a user's inputs and the resulting outputs vest in the user; the architecture, synthesis, selection, and final verification remain the Author's own original work, exactly as they would using a word processor or a compiler. A tool provider who later claims to have derived, trained on, or discovered a Corpus Artifact through its own records of an Author-tool interaction — rather than through the Registry — does not thereby escape this License, which reaches an Artifact through any channel, direct or intermediary (§1.3, §4.5); such a claim is, if anything, an admission of direct copying from an identified source, and may separately implicate that provider's own data-handling commitments or confidentiality obligations under applicable law — matters this License neither creates nor waives.

### §1 — Scope (Cinema Clause)
**1.1 Objective coverage.** Coverage is objective: every Artifact — code, texts, ontologies, schemas, models, data, architectural specifications — is in scope the moment its hash is timestamped in the Prior-Art registry (**https://zenodo.org/communities/sve**), including Artifacts registered after this License. This License governs the *distribution* of that expression — copying, adapting, running as a service — exactly as a film license governs prints and screenings, not the story. It does not claim rights copyright cannot give (bare ideas, methods, "phenomena of reality... not mentioned at all," as v5.0 §1 overreached); an undocumented idea is protected instead by Defensive Publication (§7).

**1.2 Latent Activation — click, touch, or use is consent.** This License is a unilateral public grant, not a negotiated bilateral contract: there is no counterparty to sign with, and none is needed. Rights and obligations attach the instant a person downloads, reads, runs, modifies, forwards, or offers as a Network Service any Corpus Artifact — whichever comes first — not upon signature, registration, account creation, or payment. Where an official Custodian channel presents an explicit accept-or-click step, that click is simply the clearest evidence of what Latent Activation already accomplishes; its absence changes nothing. This is Core Principle 4 of Meta-License v4.0 (Latent Activation), made explicit and operative here, with the same legal ancestry v4.0's own Legal Ancestors table already cites: clickwrap enforceability (*ProCD, Inc. v. Zeidenberg*, 86 F.3d 1447 (7th Cir. 1996)) and GPL-style copyleft, which binds on use, not on signature.

**1.3 Channel independence — the Cinema Clause applied to piracy.** Because §1.1 makes coverage a fact about the Artifact's hash, not about how a given copy reached a given Recipient, obtaining a Corpus Artifact through an unauthorized, unofficial, or "pirate" channel — a mirror, a forward, a third-party repost, a leak — does **not** exempt the Recipient from this License. It places them inside it, on identical terms to a Recipient who obtained it from the Registry directly: exactly as watching a pirated copy of a film does not exempt the viewer from copyright, or grant them any right the licensed viewer lacks. A party who strips or omits required Provenance notices (§4.3) is independently liable for that breach; stripping notices does not free anyone further downstream — the Registry's public, hash-addressable record stands as constructive notice regardless, on the same footing as a patent register. There is no clean-hands exit from this License through an unofficial copy; there is only compliance or breach.

### §2 — Who May Hold This License (Natural-Person Rule)
**2.1** Only a natural person — a living human being — may be a Recipient under §3 and exercise rights directly under this License. A legal entity (corporation, LLC, partnership, foundation, government body, trust, DAO treasury, or similar) cannot itself hold, receive, or exercise rights under this License.

**2.2 Operating through an entity.** A legal entity may still build and operate a product or service on Corpus Artifacts, but only through at least one specific, named natural person (a **Sponsor**) who personally accepts this License in their own name and remains personally, individually, and without limitation liable for that entity's compliance — audited, terminated, and subject to Appendix F consequences in their own name, regardless of any corporate or limited-liability shield. If a Sponsor departs, dies, or is removed, the entity must name a successor Sponsor within 90 days or its grant lapses.

**2.3 Outside SVE.** This does not touch the entity's ordinary contracts, corporate structure, or limited liability for anything *outside* this License — a company remains free to organize itself however it likes. It simply cannot use that structure to place its SVE compliance behind a wall no one is personally answerable for. A closed circle of liability is itself a form of enclosure (§5).

**2.4 Identifiability.** A Sponsor may act under a pseudonym only if that pseudonym is traceable, on the same terms as any other Recipient under §8's audit, to a real, accountable person — an unreachable or purely anonymous Sponsor is no Sponsor, and the grant does not attach.

**2.5 Payment is not licensure.** A legal entity — including the SVE Foundation, a community treasury, or a payment processor — may still receive funds under §10's revenue-share without itself being a Recipient: accepting money is not exercising rights under §3.

**2.6 Not the SVE governance apparatus.** This section governs Recipients — those receiving and exercising rights under §3. It does not require the Custodian, the Veche, the DVN, the SVE Foundation, or other SVE governance bodies to name a Sponsor for their own governance functions; it governs who may *use* the Corpus, not the bodies that steward it.

### §3 — Grant
Each Recipient receives a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive license, continuing during compliance, to reproduce, distribute, adapt, and run as a Network Service any Corpus Artifact — **in any field of endeavor, including commercial use, without prior authorization** — subject to §§4–10.

### §4 — Anti-Enclosure (Share-Alike / Network Copyleft)
**4.1** Distribute or adapt only under this License; no additional restriction, no relicensing into closed terms.

**4.2 Network Service disclosure.** Whoever runs a Network Service materially relying on a Corpus Artifact must make available to that Service's users the corresponding modifications — source code for software; full schemas, ontologies, parameters, and model weights for non-code Artifacts.

**4.3 Provenance notices.** Every copy or redistribution, official or not, must preserve the Artifact's License reference, its Registry hash, and a link to **https://zenodo.org/communities/sve**. A party who strips, alters, or omits these notices is independently liable for that breach (§8), and gains no shelter from it — it does not free anyone further downstream from this License (see §1.3).

**4.4** No technical or contractual measure may defeat any of §§4.1–4.3.

**4.5 AI training is adaptation; TDM exception opted out.** Using a Corpus Artifact — in whole or in part, alone or blended with other material — to train, fine-tune, distill, or otherwise derive the parameters of a machine-learning model is an adaptation under §4.1 and creates a Derivative, triggering §4.2's disclosure duty (full parameters/weights) regardless of whether any individual weight can be isolated or "traced back" to a specific input: neural-network weight entanglement is not a defense to Derivative status. What matters is inclusion of the Artifact in the training process, not extractability of the output. The Author expressly opts out, to the maximum extent permitted, of any Text-and-Data-Mining exception that would otherwise permit this without a license — including under Article 4 of EU Directive 2019/790 — exercising the Article 4(3) reservation mechanism; this reservation is expressed in this text and, where technically supported, in machine-readable form at the Registry. A Recipient's own internal characterization of the use — "research only," "safety testing," "not production," a log entry describing limited use — does not itself exempt that use from this section where the objective fact of training-data inclusion is established by any evidence: the Recipient's own records, a dataset manifest, third-party disclosure, or demonstrated model behavior. §1.3's channel-independence rule applies identically here: an Artifact that reached a training corpus indirectly — via a third-party dataset, a scrape-of-a-scrape, or an intermediary redistributor — does not escape coverage. This section reflects a principle wider than any one Artifact: a system that already draws on humanity's collective intellectual output owes reciprocal openness in equal measure — the Corpus is simply the hash-verifiable instance of that principle this License can enforce directly.

**4.6 Discovery duty.** A Recipient who discovers — through their own logs, an internal audit, external research, or otherwise — that a Corpus Artifact was used, directly or via an intermediary, in training a model they still operate or distribute, must bring that product or service into compliance with this License (disclosure, revenue-share, and all other applicable duties) from the date of discovery forward. Continuing to operate without disclosing a known prior inclusion is concealment; concealment after discovery is independently a Fruits Test breach auditable under §8, regardless of whether the original training predates this License.

**4.7 No Secondary Secrecy.** Maxim II ("live by your skills and talents — not by your secrets") is operative, not decorative. A Recipient's own private matters — what they alone know, touching no other person's use of a Corpus Artifact — are entirely outside this License's reach: privacy is not enclosure, and nothing here asks anyone to live without one. The line sits exactly where a second party enters the picture: the moment a Corpus Artifact is used, built on, modified, or operated in a way that involves anyone beyond the Recipient's own person — an employer, a client, a Service's users, a counterparty, a collaborator — the transparency §§4.1–4.6 and §5 already require applies in full; there is no "confidential" or "NDA-protected" tier of SVE use, and dressing enclosure up as a business-confidentiality term is not a lesser violation. A Recipient or Sponsor who needs standing secrecy toward another party about what a Corpus Artifact is, does, or was used for should not use the Corpus at all — that need is itself incompatible with this License, not a lesser breach of it.

This does not forbid ordinary, disclosed, time-bound collaboration: coworkers and contractors building something together ahead of a scheduled, registered disclosure — including under SVE-Unicorns Founder Mode's freeze (§11) — are not "secondary secrecy" in the sense this section targets; the target is secrecy that serves enclosure, a permanent or indefinite wall against the outside world, not the ordinary process of building something with other people before telling everyone else. Nor does it reach a party's confidentiality about matters genuinely unrelated to a Corpus Artifact's use (an unrelated vendor NDA, unrelated trade secrets, an employee's compensation), or privileged legal advice about how to comply with this License — consulting counsel about SVE obligations privileges the analysis, not the underlying Corpus-touching facts, which remain fully disclosable. Where secrecy is not chosen but legally compelled — a court seal, a gag order, a lawful government demand — §6.3's withdrawal logic applies by analogy: continuing to operate the affected product or service under this License while concealing what compliance requires disclosing is not permitted, but withdrawing it from this License for the duration of the compulsion is. Protecting personal data or a vulnerable individual's privacy (§9.5) is never "secrecy" in the sense this section forbids — it protects a person, not an enclosure.

**4.8 Reading is free; incorporation is not — no status-based exception.** Nothing in this License restricts who may read, study, index, or discuss a Corpus Artifact: a closed-source AI system, a proprietary search engine, or any other party may inspect it exactly as any human reader may, without triggering any duty — ideas and inspection remain free under §1.1, and Universal Access (§6.2) admits no exception for anyone, closed-source companies included; this section adds no new exception either. What §4.5 regulates is not who looks, but what happens next: a system — open or closed — that trains on, fine-tunes with, or otherwise incorporates a Corpus Artifact into its operative parameters, while keeping the resulting parameters closed, is in the same full, uncured breach of §4.2/§4.5 as any other undisclosed derivative; there is no separate, lesser "closed-use" tier, and no discount for size or market position. A closed system cannot, as a practical matter, train on the Corpus and remain closed for that part without breaching this License — but that is the ordinary disclosure duty applying symmetrically to everyone, the same way it always has, not a new carve-out into §6.2 aimed at anyone in particular. Indexing, by any search engine regardless of its own licensing model, is not itself an adaptation and needs no license; only a synthesis or answer feature built from indexed Corpus content that materially relies on it is a Network Service under §4.2, subject to the same disclosure duty as any other derivative.

### §5 — Wholeness Clause (No Partial Enclosure)
You may not take one part of the Corpus (say, an ontology) open while keeping what you built on top of it closed, or vice versa — the obligations of §4 attach to the whole integrated work. Nor may you accept the grant of §3 while declining the conditions of §§4–10: the two are one instrument, and partial acceptance is void. A breach here is a One-Bit violation (Appendix F §1 of Meta-License v4.0) — immediate termination.

### §6 — Ethical-Use Floor & Universal Access (Radical Symmetry)
**6.1 Absolute bars.** No tier, payment, or transparency log cures the following — void *ab initio* regardless of any other term: **(a)** lethal autonomous weapons or targeting systems; **(b)** mass covert surveillance or repression without due process; **(c)** disinformation or psychological-manipulation campaigns at scale; **(d)** any exploitation of minors.

**6.2 Universal Access — absolute.** A product or service built on a Corpus Artifact must be offered on the same terms to every person and entity on Earth. **No exception** for nationality, citizenship, geography, political affiliation, sanctions status, government designation, or religion. *"He makes His sun rise on the evil and on the good, and sends rain on the just and on the unjust."* — Matthew 5:45. This License claims no lesser standard, and none is read into it. This gives absolute, unconditional operative force to Commandment 6 of the SVE-Unicorns Patch and to Core Principle 2, Radical Symmetry (Meta-License v4.0 §2.2).

**6.3 No selective withdrawal.** This License has no power to instruct anyone to break a law that actually binds them, and does not try. What it does not permit is *selective* compliance: serving some and withholding from others while continuing to operate the same product or service under this License. An operator who cannot lawfully serve everyone under §6.2 has exactly one path that keeps this License in force: **withdraw that product or service from this License entirely, for everyone, for as long as the conflict stands** — never carve out the forbidden party while keeping the rest of the market. Serving everyone but one is a §6.2 breach. Serving no one under SVE terms is not.

**6.4 Conduct, not identity.** §6.2 bars turning someone away for *who they are*. It does not protect *what they do*: an operator may suspend a specific user for confirmed bad-fruits conduct under §8.2, following the same audit-first sequencing as §8.3. That is a conduct-based distinction, not a status-based one, and does not implicate this section — Matthew 5:45's symmetry governs standing to receive, not license to harm.

**6.5 Transparency & audit.** Any withdrawal under §6.3 must be publicly logged (what, why, since when) and is subject to §8's Reciprocal-Audit-Priority rule on challenge. A claimed §6.3 withdrawal that turns out to still be serving some users while excluding others is the §6.2 breach it actually is — not a lesser matter.

**6.6 Symmetry cuts both ways.** §6.2–6.5 bind identically no matter whose politics or sanctions are at issue, and identically to the Custodian's own ventures, including any under SVE-Unicorns Founder Mode — Radical Symmetry means the Custodian binds himself first.

**6.7 Non-severable — and no fallback substitute.** Because §6.2 restates Core Principle 2 (Radical Symmetry), which Meta-License v4.0 §2 makes immutable, §6.2–§6.5 stand on the same non-severable footing as §0 and the Core Principles: no court may narrow them piecemeal. If held unenforceable somewhere, the remainder is read to maximize alignment with them; only if that is impossible does this License become void in that jurisdiction — see §14. That voidance, where it occurs, is contained to that jurisdiction only, not spread worldwide (§0.2's standalone-validity principle runs equally in this direction). But within that jurisdiction, there is no consolation prize: the Dormant Sliding Fallback (§12) does **not** reach §6, at any rung, for that product or service. A fallback that let an operator keep operating on lesser terms while still excluding whoever the local restriction targets would itself be exactly the selective compliance §6.3 already forbids, arrived at through a side door. If a given product or service cannot honor §6 in a given jurisdiction, no grant of any kind — primary or fallback — subsists there for it: not AGPL, not CC BY-SA, nothing. §6.3's own all-or-nothing withdrawal remains the only lawful way back in. Human dignity is not a copyright condition to be downgraded; it is the floor everything else stands on.

**6.8 Consequence.** A breach of §6.1 or §6.2 is an Appendix F betrayal case: immediate termination of all rights under this License, no cure, no repentance path.

### §7 — Patent & Enclosure Shield
The Registry is a public, timestamped disclosure and prior art against private patenting of what it describes. Patent-holders among Recipients license compliant users to practice their patents for Corpus purposes; initiating patent litigation, or enclosing a Derivative, terminates that actor's rights (curable within 30 days of notice; not curable on repeat). Where EU or equivalent law grants a *sui generis* database right or other ancillary right in the Registry or a dataset Artifact, the Custodian exercises it under these same terms — an added layer, never a separate closed one.

### §8 — Fruits Test & Reciprocal Audit Priority ("Glass House Rule")
**8.1** §0 of Meta-License v4.0 (Fruits Test) remains supreme, unweakened — the one standard everything else in this License exists to serve.

**8.2 End-User Fruits Duty.** The Fruits Test does not stop at the operator. Anyone who *uses* a product or service built on a Corpus Artifact — not only the Recipient who built it — must apply that use toward good fruits: benefit, not harm, to persons, non-human beings, and ecosystems. Use toward bad fruits is grounds for a suspicion-triggered audit under §8.3, reaching the end-user directly, not only whoever served them.

**8.3 Reciprocal priority.** Whoever initiates a suspicion-based audit or breach report — against a Recipient, a Sponsor, or an end-user — must submit to the same audit, on the same subject, **first or at the same time — never after** (elevating and sharpening Appendix G §3A). A refusal voids the audit request; the initiator bears its cost; a repeated bad-faith demand triggers the Anti-Judas Protocol (Appendix F §6, 3× penalty).

**8.4 Asymmetric Rigor ("Narrow Gate").** This License is deliberately asymmetric. For a Recipient with clean hands and good fruits, its burdens amount to almost nothing: no permission needed, no fee for research or truth-bearing use, and a suspicion-audit a compliant party can simply answer. For a Recipient shown to have acted in bad faith — willful concealment, willful fabrication, selective compliance, or exploitation — the same mechanisms apply at full and uncompromising strength: no cure period where §6.8 already withholds one, the full weight of the One-Bit Rule and the Anti-Judas Protocol's 3× penalty, and no discount for good conduct elsewhere — prior compliance does not offset a proven present breach. A Recipient who refuses to cooperate with a properly-initiated audit under §8.3, or who obstructs, delays, or conceals records from one, is treated as having supplied the adverse evidence itself. None of this is vindictive: restoration remains open through Appendix F's Betrayal & Restoration Protocol on the same terms it always has — full transparency, no minimization — this section only makes explicit that the path is narrow, not that it is closed (cf. Matthew 7:13–14).

### §9 — Research, Investigation & Truth-Bearer Use
**9.1 Research is already free — here it is explicit.** Academic, scientific, and independent research use of any Corpus Artifact — including failed, partial, negative, or null-result experiments — needs no separate permission and no separate fee beyond §10's ordinary terms: it is already covered by the Grant (§3) and Anti-Enclosure (§4) like any other use. This section adds what research specifically needs: a duty to publish what was actually found (§9.2), and a rule against faking it (§9.3).

**9.2 Field-Notes Duty.** A Recipient who conducts research using a Corpus Artifact — funded or unfunded, academic, independent, or corporate R&D — must make available a field-notes record of that research: methodology, and both positive and negative/null results of material experiments performed with or on the Artifact. This extends §4.2's disclosure duty to the research process itself: publicizing a positive result while burying a negative one is enclosure by omission, and as much a §4 breach as hiding source code. Publication may be reasonably delayed — for safety review, peer review, or a fixed embargo disclosed in advance — but indefinite or permanent suppression of an inconvenient result is not a delay; it is a breach.

**9.3 Research Integrity.** Deliberate, willful fabrication of results, material misrepresentation of what an Artifact was found to do, or knowingly withholding what is needed to reproduce a claimed result is a Fruits Test breach, auditable on suspicion under §8.3 exactly like any other bad fruit. Honest error, a good-faith failed replication, or a negative result honestly reported are not breaches — they are precisely the field notes §9.2 exists to capture. Intent is what separates science that didn't work from science that lied.

**9.4 Truth-Bearer Track.** Anyone using a Corpus Artifact to investigate, document, verify, or expose matters of genuine public interest — journalists, whistleblowers, human-rights investigators, and similar accountability work — may do so at no cost and outside §10's revenue-share, on the same footing as non-commercial research, even where the resulting work is itself monetized (e.g., published journalism carrying advertising): the exposure and accountability *is* the fruit this License asks for. This track carries the same duties as §9.2–§9.3: methodology and substantiated findings must eventually be made available, and deliberate fabrication is a breach exactly as in §9.3.

**9.5 Source protection is not enclosure.** §9.2's and §9.4's disclosure duties never require identifying a confidential source, informant, or vulnerable subject whose safety depends on protection — disclosing methodology and substantiated findings while protecting a source's identity satisfies both duties in full. Protecting the vulnerable is itself a good fruit under §8.1, not a shortfall against it.

**9.6 Not a license to defame.** §9.4 covers good-faith investigation, not a label for harassment, exposure of private individuals absent genuine public interest, or knowingly false claims dressed as "investigation" — those remain barred by §6.1(c) and gain no cover from this section.

### §10 — Commercial Use & Revenue-Share
Commercial use of any Corpus Artifact is permitted without prior authorization, subject to §§4–9 and the revenue-share tiers of Meta-License v4.0 Appendix B. **v5.0 §3's residual CC BY-NC-SA (Non-Commercial) fallback is withdrawn** and replaced by §§4–6 above — see §0 for why this strengthens rather than weakens the framework.

### §11 — SVE-Unicorns Founder Mode (Cross-Reference)
For founder-stage ventures under the size thresholds of Founder Mode (≤$100M revenue, ≤1,000 staff, ≤$500M cap), the **SVE-Unicorns Patch v1.0** ("Founder Mode," signed 26.06.2026) still stands **unchanged** — this version does not reissue it, and needs no Sponsor mechanism grafted on: the founder is already, by the Patch's own design, the single named, personally-bound individual §2 now requires framework-wide — "the Custodian binds himself first" already assumed exactly this. In plain terms: a qualifying founder gets total operational freedom and may *freeze* — defer publication of — their methods until Year 44, without that freeze counting as enclosure, provided the 6 Commandments hold (audited on suspicion, per §8 above) and the work is hash-registered as SVE-bound from day one. To remove any doubt this version could create: a frozen-but-registered Artifact is **not** in breach of §4's disclosure duty or §5's Wholeness Clause — freezing defers *when* you must disclose, it does not exempt you from *that* you must eventually disclose, may never enclose, and may never sell the company. Cross the size thresholds, and Founder Mode merges into the standard 44-Year Corporate Path (Appendix B §§10–22), where the Sponsor role is naturally the CEO or equivalent officer of record.

### §12 — Resilience: Dormant Sliding Fallback
Where a §3 grant, or a §4, §5, or §7 condition, is held unenforceable for a given Recipient or jurisdiction, it is replaced — for that case only — by the strongest enforceable equivalent: **L1** AGPL-3.0 (software) / CC BY-SA 4.0 (non-software); **L2** Apache-2.0 (retaining §7's patent-license and anti-aggression terms) / CC BY 4.0; **L3** attribution + the §7 covenant + continued availability. These are real, independent grants that stay dormant until the primary SVE grant actually fails for that case — never chosen, never stacked on top of a working primary grant. Because §10 removes the NC restriction, L1 no longer conflicts with the primary grant the way v5.0's cascade did — this is what fixes it. **This Ladder never reaches §6 (Ethical-Use Floor & Universal Access) or §8 (Fruits Test) at any rung** — see §6.7. Those are not copyright mechanics with a swappable equivalent; a failure there is a termination or a full withdrawal, never a downgrade to a lesser license that would let the underlying problem continue under a different label.

### §13 — Ratchet & Retroactive Envelope
From the moment of signature, v5.5 applies retroactively to all SVE-licensed works, all prior versions, and the entire Prior-Art registry — as a strengthening only: no right already granted is diminished, nothing already open is closed. Future minor versions may issue on the same §0 terms until a Veche ratifies canonically; future major versions may only strengthen §§0–12.

### §14 — Severability
An invalid provision is severed and the remainder enforced to the fullest extent possible. §0 (Fruits Test), the Core Principles of Meta-License v4.0, §1.2–§1.3 of this License (Latent Activation and its channel-independence corollary, restating Core Principle 4), §4.7 of this License (No Secondary Secrecy, restating Core Principle 3, Zero Secrecy), §9.2–§9.3 of this License (Field-Notes Duty and Research Integrity, also restating Core Principle 3, Zero Secrecy), and §6.2–§6.5 of this License (Universal Access, restating Core Principle 2) are **non-severable**: if any is held unenforceable in a given jurisdiction, the remainder is interpreted to maximize alignment with it rather than narrowed around it; only if that is impossible does this License become void in that jurisdiction — never silently cut down to a lesser rule. §0.2's standalone-validity principle (including its moral-rights fork) and §0.3's Contribution Rule are themselves structural rather than substantive protections and are treated as inseverable from this License's basic operation — a License that could be argued out of its own standalone-validity clause would lose the very protection that clause exists to provide. §2 (Natural-Person Rule) and §9.1, §9.4–§9.6 (the Research/Truth-Bearer track's scope and safeguards) are severable on ordinary terms unless a future version elevates them.

